			<div type="bibliography">
				<!--De Beylié 1907a: 83 (n. 1). — ASB 1907, 10; 1910, 21; 1916, 18 (with n. ‡), 20, 55; 1917, 24-5; 1922, 17. — Blagden 1913-14: 127 (6). — Chen Yi-Sein 1960. — PPPB I, 67, 76 n. 38-39.-->
				<!--[Todo: markup citedRange elements if necessary]-->
				<p><bibl><ptr target="bib:Beylié1907a"/><citedRange>83 (n. 1)</citedRange></bibl> makes passing reference to this badly effaced inscription, noting that it was bilingual with one side in Chinese. <bibl><ptr target="bib:ASB"/><citedRange>1910: 21</citedRange></bibl> mistakenly identified the non-Chinese side as Mongol rather than Pyu. <bibl><ptr target="bib:ChenYiSein1960_01"/></bibl> identified about 80 characters on the Chinese side, enough to propose that the text was an imperial edict dating from approximately 1297. <bibl><ptr target="bib:Luce1985_01"/><citedRange>I, 67, 75 n. 38-39</citedRange></bibl>, estimates 25 lines for the Pyu face. <bibl><ptr target="bib:SeinWin2016_01"/><citedRange>175-85</citedRange></bibl> is the first attempt to transliterate (in Burmese script) all legible parts of the Pyu face.</p>
				<listBibl type="primary"><bibl><ptr target="bib:CPI2018_01"/><citedRange unit="item">DHARMA_INSPYU00011</citedRange></bibl></listBibl><listBibl type="secondary">
